content.thinking:
Visual analysis reveals a problem: the default view shows only ONE object node and very few region summaries in the visible slice — the visible area feels sparse/empty ("更像缩略图"), not a readable map. The camera is centered on the map center but key objects spread elsewhere. The region summaries ARE somewhere but only partially visible (bottom-left corner shows region header fragments).

Root cause: at zoom 0.5 clamp, we see ~1/3 of the map; the centered region shows mostly EMPTY space because architecture density withheld all normal objects (complex has 8 key of 64). The visible slice has 1 key object. Region summaries exist but at region top-left corners — some outside the view.

Improvements needed for "默认视图占据有效视口的大部分 + 不先缩放能读 Region 和关键 Object 标签":

Option A: Initial fit should fit the KEY content (visible objects + regions containing them)? No — regions span everything.

Option B: When the fit is clamped, center on the "key content bounds" (critical/high objects' union + enclosing region headers) rather than whole map center. That way the first screen shows the important cluster. Pan reveals the rest. This is a meaningful "smart default" consistent with "关键 Object 标签可读".

Implement: in the fit effect, compute fit target nodes = visible object nodes (view-dependent)? fitView({nodes}) accepts nodes. For the DEFAULT fit only (not user Fit button? Fit button should also be smart — same behavior). Use nodes = key objects at architecture density... but node set depends on zoom → circular. Approximation: at default, fit nodes = objects with importance critical/high + ALL region nodes? regions dominate bounds again. So fit nodes = key object nodes only, padding larger (0.3), clamped by minZoom 0.5 and maxZoom 1.05. Region frames still visible around them.

Hmm, but then map context shrinks — "不丢失全局上下文". Minimap provides global context. And regions containing key objects are visible.

Wait — alternative Option C: reduce map world size so everything fits at 0.5: complex map 3970x3780 world; viewport shows 3086x1552 world at 0.5. If map were ~3000x1500 it would fit entirely. Can't shrink member geometry (ELK internal layouts fixed sizes).

Option B is right: clamp + center on key content. For fixtures, key objects = 8 critical/high (indices 0-7). Their spread across regions ~ half the map. Fit those 8 with padding 0.25 at minZoom 0.5 → shows a meaningful readable slice containing all key objects + their region summaries.

Implementation in architecture-canvas.tsx fit effect:
```ts
const targetNodes = instance.getNodes().filter((node) => {
  if (node.type === "object") {
    const object = graph.objects.find(o => o.id === node.id)!; // expensive; use index
    return isKeyObject... 
  }
  return false;
});
```
Better: compute key object ids from graph (importance critical/high). If key set empty → fit all (existing behavior).

fitView({ nodes: keyNodes, padding: 0.28, minZoom: ARCHITECTURE_ZOOM_FLOOR, maxZoom: CANVAS_FIT_MAX_ZOOM, duration }).

But careful: after fit at 0.5 clamp centered on key bounds — key objects readable. Region summaries of regions containing them: visible if region headers near key objects... plausible.

Also the Fit button: same smart behavior (fit key objects). Users can still see whole map via zoom-out manually (allowed - user choice below floor triggers landscape... wait zooming below 0.48 → landscape → more withholding. That's by design).

Circular dependency: key nodes must be in `nodes` state (projected). At initial projection, interaction.zoom=0.72 → architecture → visible = key objects only (complex) → key nodes exist in nodes. For small fixture (≤24): all objects are nodes; key subset = 8; fitting 8 of 15 with padding → zoom higher than full fit → labels even more readable, but map context (7 normal objects) partially out of view! Hmm — that violates "small maps stay complete".

Refine: fit target = all currently-projected visible object nodes (not just key): at architecture density visible set == what the map currently shows. Small: all 15 → same as before ✓. Complex: key 8 → readable center ✓. Medium (40>24): key only ✓. The projected node set IS the semantic answer to "what should be readable now". 

So: default fit = fitView({ nodes: visible object nodes }) with clamp. If none (empty), fallback plain fitView.

This is clean: "camera fits the readable content, not all geometry". And Fit button same logic → deterministic function fitReadableMap(instance) exported? For testability, extract pure helper `readableFitNodes(graph, view)`? Tests: given view (architecture, complex) → returns key object ids. That duplicates visibleObjectIds… Just reuse: the nodes passed to kernel are already filtered. The effect can filter projected nodes by type object.

Implement in the effect and Fit button:
```ts
const objectNodesForFit = () => instanceRef.current?.getNodes().filter((n) => n.type === "object") ?? [];
const fitContract = (duration: number) => {
  const instance = instanceRef.current;
  if (!instance) return;
  const targets = objectNodesForFit();
  if (targets.length > 0) instance.fitView({ nodes: targets, padding: 0.24, ...fitViewContract(), duration });
  else instance.fitView({ ...fitViewContract(), duration });
};
```
Also trace select fit already fits flow nodes (targetNodes) ✓ and focus fit fits focus set ✓ — consistent.

Padding: key nodes with padding 0.24 gives breathing room.

Wait — one more consideration: after fit centered on key objects, onMoveEnd updates interaction.zoom to 0.5 → view recalculates → visible set stable (key objects at architecture) → nodes stable. Good, no loop.

Edge case: Browse with search → visible = matches (muted others? visibleObjectIds: searchMatches-based: important || searchMatches). Fit button then centers on matches — sensible.

Implement now.

