The served code contains everything including the console.info. So the effect IS in the code but never logs → the effect genuinely doesn't run when inspectorOpen flips from false to true... 

Wait, actually — is it possible that the click handler chain (select → setInteraction mode inspect) doesn't change `inspectorBinding`?? inspectorBinding = bindInspector(graph, interaction.selection). Selection becomes {kind:"object", id} → binding non-null → inspectorOpen true → effect [inspectorOpen] fires. And Inspector opened visually (class open, 336px)! So inspectorBinding DID become truthy. The effect MUST have fired...

Unless — the effect ran BEFORE the latest code was loaded? No, single page load.

Hmm wait, maybe the effect ran but threw before console.info? Order in effect:
```ts
const instance = instanceRef.current;
if (!instance) return;
if (!dockCompensatedRef.current) { dockCompensatedRef.current = true; return; }
const viewport = instance.getViewport();
console.info(...)
```
If `instance` null → silent return. When would instanceRef be null? onInit sets it. React Flow onInit fires at mount. Unless ArchitectureCanvasKernel remounts on mode change?? mode is a prop → className changes → no remount.

OR — `dockCompensatedRef.current` never became true before... no, first run sets it and returns silently. Then second run logs. Unless the FIRST run happened when instance was null: mount → effect runs → instanceRef.current null (React Flow onInit may fire after effects!) → `if (!instance) return;` → ref NEVER SET. Then on click: instance now set → passes null check → dockCompensatedRef.current false → sets true → returns silently! THAT'S THE BUG. The first-run skip logic is broken because at first run the instance was null, so the "compensated" flag was never consumed.

Fix: track "first actual compensation-capable run":
```ts
if (!instance) return;
if (!dockCompensatedRef.current) {
  dockCompensatedRef.current = true;
  return;
}
```
→ change to only skip when inspectorOpen === false:
Actually purpose of skip: don't shift on initial mount (dock starts closed, no change). The shift only matters when inspectorOpen CHANGES. Use a ref storing previous state:

```ts
const prevInspectorOpenRef = useRef(false);
useEffect(() => {
  const instance = instanceRef.current;
  if (!instance) { prevInspectorOpenRef.current = inspectorOpen; return; }
  if (prevInspectorOpenRef.current === inspectorOpen) return;
  prevInspectorOpenRef.current = inspectorOpen;
  const viewport = instance.getViewport();
  instance.setViewport(...);
}, [inspectorOpen]);
```
This shifts only on actual transitions, regardless of instance timing. Edge: click before init impossible (canvas empty).

Remove console.info too.

